  "result": "{\"seen_before\": true, \"matches\": [\"terminal#16236\", \"terminal#14185\", \"terminal#16765\"], \"answer\": \"This looks like a known problem on Windows 10 (build 19045). Explorer sometimes loses or never loads the Terminal shell-extension entry, and reinstalling doesn't always bring it back. The archived tickets give no confirmed fix. They do suggest the following:\\n\\n1. Restart Explorer, which caches context-menu entries. Run `taskkill /f /im explorer.exe && explorer.exe`, or reboot.\\n2. On Windows 10, right-clicking the empty background of a folder, the desktop or a drive often doesn't show the entry. Right-click an actual folder instead.\\n3. As a workaround, add a registry entry. Under `HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\\\\Directory\\\\Background\\\\shell\\\\wt\\\\command`, set the default value to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\\\Microsoft\\\\WindowsApps\\\\wt.exe -d \\\"%V\\\\.\\\"`. Treat this as a workaround only, because in #16765 a similar manual entry did not open anything.\\n4. If it still doesn't work, send feedback through Feedback Hub under \\\"Desktop Environment > Right-click context menu\\\". The maintainers believe this is an OS-side bug. They track the issue in #14185, so add your details and a thumbs-up there.\"}",
